      "writtenOn": "2021-09-27T23:14:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Under Zephyr, drivers are typically initialized before the kernel, but runtime use requires that the kernel is initialized.\n\nZephyr does have an upstream bit-bang I2C driver, but it isn\u0027t designed work using the same pins as the SoC native driver (no run-time pin configuration).  A quick look through the bit-bang driver and I don\u0027t see kernel services used, but the underlying GPIO driver might use them.\n\nThis approach of using both bit-bang and the native will likely take same significant work to make functional under Zephyr.  Can the keyboard IC get enabled after tasks and the kernel are running?",
      "parentUuid": "ed7d89bc_2562ea58",
      "revId": "a9a48642c19d86d04aec66ff9577e9ae09361d87",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "10d3c890_b16acff4",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132339
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-28T05:55:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In Zephyr, EC App is executed after I2C driver and kernel initialization. It should be no problem to use native I2C driver in the ec_app_main.c. I have verified it with npcx_evb by issuing I2C transaction here:\nhttps://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/refs/heads/main/zephyr/app/ec/ec_app_main.c#54",
      "parentUuid": "f91d9cb1_fa4bd56e",
      "revId": "a9a48642c19d86d04aec66ff9577e9ae09361d87",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
